The most natural way to model uncertainty in a computer program is for it produce values in a indeterministic way, a process we refer to as _sampling_.
From the perspective of a typed language, we would like to offer functionality for users to simulate seemingly random outcomes for all sorts of types--e.g. `bool`, `[Card; 5]`, or `Vec<f64>`.
This subsection will dive into how we will achieve this.

# Pseudorandom number generation

The simplest utility we can offer is that of a _pseudorandom number generator_ which acts as a source for users to randomly (at least seemingly) generate bytes for their type `T`.

<Statement {...rngDef} />

<Statement {...rngDefRemark} />

A true random number generator could be implemented by reading voltages from an analog-to-digital circuit, since quantum physics dictates that there is a true source of randomness present in the electronics.
However, the nature of this randomness could vary between hardware devices and be quite difficult to characterize.
In principle, we would like our psueodorandom number generator to maximize _surprise_ or _entropy_, in the sense that the $2^8$ options of a byte are all seemingly equally likely to occur with repeated calls and that the sequence of outputs induced by repeated calls do not exhibit any recognizable pattern.
If someone can guess the value of the next call `rng.next_u32()` given the history of its earlier outcomes, it would no longer be random to them.

> **Note.** This sequence will reserve the term _entropy_ to precisely mean the intuitive definition above.
> While entropy _does_ have a probabilistic definition in information theory, we would somehow be begging the question if we defined it rigorously at this moment.

Thankfully, it turns out this property of psueodorandom number generators is extremely important in cryptography, so there are many algorithms at our disposal.
Most of these implementations are hard to reason through, but only because they are secure and highly optimized for hardware; in principle, they only typically involve performing simple addition and bitwise operations on `u32` instances.
In this sequence, we will not worry about implementing `Rng` as all subsequent objects will only interface with the trait; instead we will use the Cargo crate [rand](https://docs.rs/rand/latest/rand/index.html) for implementations of random number generators `Rng`.

```rs
// this is how we will get instances `rng` of some type `R` which implements `Rng`
let mut rng = rand::rng();
```

# Probability measures

Returning to the task at hand, we define a probability measure (for now) as the object which serves our sampling desires.

<Statement {...prbDef} />

<Statement {...prbDefRemark} />

With this (currently) simple interface, we can construct probability measures to our liking by coming up with different sampling mechanisms.
Since the randomness comes from the underlying bytes produced by the pseudorandom number generator `R`, if we assume the implementation of `R` samples the $2^8$ options of a byte evenly, then we can intuitively produce desired behaviors in the probability measure implementation `ProbabilityMeasure<R>`.

## Dirac measure

The simplest probability measure is one which exhibits no randomness.
In other words, the sampling mechanism is determinisitic and constant.

<Statement {...dirac} />

Note how the `Dirac` measure is a pretty useless probability measure as far as building programs goes.

```rs
let mut rng = rand::rng();
let dirac = Dirac::new(1.0);
let x = dirac.sample(&mut rng);
assert_eq!(x, 1.0); // cool, its 1.0
let x = dirac.sample(&mut rng);
assert_eq!(x, 1.0); // cool, its 1.0 again!
let x = dirac.sample(&mut rng);
assert_eq!(x, 1.0); // ... wait, why am I sampling if it is always 1.0?
```

## Fair Bernoulli measure

Is a probability measure with only one sample too simple?
Let's create one with **two** samples!

<Statement {...fairBernoulli} />

We see that the implementation leverages the entropy from `Rng::next_u32` and reads only the least significant bit to produce `true` or `false`.
This pattern of leveraging methods from `Rng` to produce entropy should be used in every implementation of `ProbabilityMeasure<R>`.
Let's use this implementation to simulate a coin flip!

<CoinFlip />

```
{../code/src/sampling/examples.rs:5..8}
```

If we wanted to simulate an _unfair_ coin that is still indeterministic, we could instead construct a `Bernoulli` measure, which depends on a parameter $p \in [0, 1]$ deciding the proportion of outcomes which return `true` on repeated applications.
If we assume every one of the $2^{64}$ instances of `u64` output by `Rng::next_u64` is equally likely (due to a maximal entropy), then we could use the ordering of `u64` to map roughly $p 2^{64}$ instances to `true` and $(1 - p)2^{64}$ instances to `false`.
Of course, this implementation would require $p$ is encoded with a floating point number like `f64`, so to implement this algorithm, we need a good understanding of how floats are encoded.

## IEEE-754

The `f64` type encodes the real numbers $\bbR$ with $64$ bits according to the [IEEE-754 standard](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double-precision_floating-point_format).
Given a bit sequence $(b_0, \ldots, b_{63}) \in \{0, 1\}^{64}$, the associated floating point $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0})$ is calculated like so.
$$
  \begin{aligned}
    f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0}) &= (-1)^{b_{63}} 2^{E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) - 1023} \Big( 1 + d(b_{51} \cdots b_{0}) \Big) \\
    E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) &= \sum_{i=0}^{10} b_{52+i} 2^i \\
    d(b_{51} \cdots b_{0}) &= \sum_{i=1}^{52} b_{52-i} 2^{-i}
  \end{aligned}
$$
This encoding of floating-point numbers $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0}) \in \bbR$ can be intuitively understood as partitioning the real numbers $\bbR$ first into diadic intervals $[2^k, 2^{k+1})$ and $(-2^{k+1}, -2^k]$ for integers $k$, choosing finitely many of these intervals ($12$ bits worth of them), and distributing $52$ bits of `f64` instances to each such interval.
From the equations above, we see that the bits $b_{63}\cdots b_{0}$ determine the `f64` instance $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0})$ as such.

- The most significant bit $b_{63}$ determines the sign of the float $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0})$.
- The next $11$ most significant bits decide an _exponent_ integer value $0 \leq E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) \leq 2047$ which determines the integer scale $-1023 \leq k \leq 1024$ of the diadic interval $f(b_{63} \cdots b_{0}) \in [2^k, 2^{k+1})$ or $f(b_{63}, \ldots, b_{0}) \in (-2^{k+1}, -2^k]$, depending on the sign.
  $$
    k = E(b_{62} \cdots b_{52}) - 1023
  $$
- The $52$ least significant bits decide the _fractional_ or _decimal_ part $d(b_{51} \cdots b_{0}) \in [1, 2)$, which serves to evenly distribute floats within the diadic interval.

<div style:overflow-x="auto">{@html iee754}</div>

This `f64` encoding can be thought of as an equidistant placement of numbers within numerous binary orders of magnitude: each diadic interval $[2^k, 2^{k+1})$ or $(-2^{k+1}, -2^k]$ has $2^{52}$ evenly distributed numbers.

As the name _floating_ suggests, `f64` technically encodes equivalence classes of intervals in the extended reals $\bbR \cup \{-\infty, \infty\}$ as opposed to specific points.
That is to say, numbers of extremely large magnitude are identified with infinite values, while the remainder of numbers are identified with a point representable by a binary expansion as $f(b_{63}\cdots b_{0})$ above.
For example, if we want to know the `f64` representation of $x \in \bbR$, we perform the following.

- Get a bit value associated to its sign.
  $$
    b_{63} = \left\{\begin{array}{rl} 1 & x \geq 0 \\ 0 & x < 0 \end{array}\right.
  $$
- Calculate the binary order of magnitude.
  $$
    k = \lfloor\log_2|x|\rfloor
  $$
  All $k \geq 1024$ get assigned to $\pm\infty$, which has the encoding $f(b_{63}1\cdots10\cdots0)$.
  All $k < -1023$ get assigned to $\pm0$, which has the encoding $f(b_{63}0\cdots0)$.
  All $-1023 \leq k < 1024$ are then associated with the respective $11$-bit binary expansion of the exponent part.
  $$
    E(b_{62}\cdots b_{52}) = k + 1023 \in \{0, \ldots, 2047\}
  $$
- For those numbers $x$ with modest binary orders of magnitude $-1023 \leq k < 1024$, we consider the fractional part of the binary order of magnitude.
  $$
     r = |x|2^{-k} \in [1, 2)
  $$
  From here, we get the binary representation $d(b_{51}\cdots b_{0})$ of the greatest integer $m$ such that $2^{-52}m < r - 1$.
  That is to say, the bits $b_{51}\cdots b_{0}$ are decided so that
  $$
    r - 1 = d(b_{51}\cdots b_{0}) + \epsilon = \sum_{i=1}^{52} b_{52-i} 2^{-i} + \epsilon
  $$
  where $\epsilon \in [0, 2^{-52})$.
  This decides our encoding $f(b_{63}\cdots b_{0})$.

From cardinalities, it is no surprise that our map $\bbR \cup \{-\infty, \infty\} \rightarrow \{0, 1\}^{64}$ is non-injective.
However, despite the fact that the encoding intuitively assigns equal amounts of real numbers in each binary order of magnitude, we clearly see some interesting and unavoidable artifacts.
For instance, there is such a thing as
$$
  -0 = f(10\cdots0) \neq f(0\cdots0) = 0
$$
and the encodings $f(b_{63}1\cdots 1 b_{51} \cdots b_{0})$ are deemed _not-a-number_ `NaN` for bit sequences $b_{51}\cdots b_{0} \neq 0\cdots 0$.

## (Unfair) Bernoulli measure

Lets return back to our goal of creating a `Bernoulli` measure which depends on a parameter $p \in [0, 1]$.
For each $x \in \bbR$, denote the value $\lfloor x \rfloor \in \bbZ$ to be the largest integer which is no greater than $x$.
As we said, a natural way to leverage the entropy of `Rng::next_u64` is to map $0, \ldots, \lfloor p 2^{64} \rfloor$ to `true` and $\lfloor p 2^{64} \rfloor + 1, \ldots, 2^{64} - 1$ to `false`.
Thankfully, `f64` holds numbers as large as $2^{64}$, so we can calculate $\lfloor p2^{64} \rfloor$ in our implementation.

<Statement {...bernoulli} />

<Statement {...bernoulliRemark} />

With this, we make a small program which samples many outcomes of the `Bernoulli` measure; the following example illustrates that the underlying parameter is empirically detectable via observing the proportion of outcomes which turn out to be heads.
The bar-chart below shows this empirical proportion alongside the parameter $p$ of the `Bernoulli` measure.

<CoinFlipEmpirical />

