```

## Statistics

Notice how the empirical proportion `p_hat` returned by `bernoulli_sample_statistic`, i.e.
```rs
let p_hat = bernoulli_sample_statistic(p, sample_count);
```
is indeterministic (from the `Rng`) and is often times close to the parameter `p`; this shows us that our implementation of `Bernoulli` is behaving as we intuitively understand it.
We also see that the frequency with which the output `hat_p` is close to `p` increases with the `sample_count`.
Additionally, `hat_p` will necessarily look like `n / sample_count` for some integer `n`, so for small `sample_count` it may be _impossible_ for `hat_p == p` (e.g. `sample_count == 1 && p != 0 && p != 1`).

The question of **how often** `p_hat` is within some neighborhood of `p` is something we cannot really answer, as it would depend on the underlying implementation of `Rng`.
However, if we were to somehow have **theoretical** maximal entropy of `Rng`, whatever this may mean, we could see there existing a theory for answering this question.
This example fits into a more general set of problems we can pose in code as trying to minimimze `simulation::cost_of_guess` below.

```rs
/// This is a measure with which we are very familiar.
pub struct SomeMeasure {
    pub parameter: P
}
impl ProbabilityMeasure<R> for SomeMeasure {
    type Sample = X;
    fn sample(&self, rng: &mut R) -> X {
        // some implementation we know...
    }
}

/// This is a function which maps outcomes of `SomeMeasure`.
pub fn observation(x: X) -> F {
    // some implementation we know...
}

/// This gives a cost between two instances of the parameter type.
pub fn cost(p: P, p_hat: P) -> f64 {
    // some implementation we know...
}

/// Randomly generate samples from an instance of `SomeMeasure`, map them via
/// `observation`, and collect all the observations.
pub fn get_observations(rng: &mut R, parameter: P, sample_count: usize) -> Vec<F> {
    let some_measure = SomeMeasure { parameter };
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(sample_count);
    for _ in 0..sample_count {
        out.push(observation(some_measure.sample(rng)));
    }
    out
}

/// TODO:
/// We need to come up with an implementation of this which does a good job
/// of keeping the cost below low.
pub fn estimator(observations: Vec<F>) -> P {
    todo!()
}

pub mod simulation {
    const SOME_P: P = // some value we *do not* know

    /// Sample observations from `SomeMeasure` with an unknown parameter, use
    /// our estimator to guess the parameter, and evaluate the cost of our guess.
    ///
    /// Note that, though `cost` may be pure, we do not know the output of this
    /// function, on account of the indeterminism of `get_observations`.
    pub fn cost_of_guess(sample_count: usize) -> f64 {
        let mut rng: R = // some arbitrary random number generator...
        let observations = get_observations(&mut rng, SOME_P, sample_count);
        let p_hat = estimator(observations);
        cost(SOME_P, p_hat)
    }
}
```

Solving this general problem is **precisely** the task of statistics: to use mapped samples of an unknown probability measure to determine its underlying parameters.
Not until we bridge a philosophical gap between the indeterminism implied by the impurities of `ProbabilityMeasure<R>` and the pure constructs of mathematics (i.e. have a theoretical notion for entropy) could we come up with a rigorous framework which can help us answer such questions.

You or someone you know likely has taken a course in statistics and despised it.
Introductory statistics typically involves memorizing implementations of `estimator` and subsequent _tests_ which help us determine the effectiveness of `p_hat` at estimating `p`.
Memorizing these estimators and their tests can feel rather unsubstantiated when foundational questions remain unanswered, such as what it means to "observe" samples and how these tests are derived from facts about `SomeMeasure`.

I purposefully posed the general statistical problem in terms of the trait `ProbabilityMeasure<R>` as opposed to using mathematical notation to avoid the need for answering what it means to "observe".
However, we will eventually have a rigorous formulation of statistics, as soon as we do so for `ProbabilityMeasure<R>`.
This will require us to build more intuition with the programs we can build with this trait.

## Uniform measure

The next measure we introduce is the `Uniform` measure, which seeks to maximize entropy across an interval $[a, b]$.
Recall that this means to us that the outputs of its sampling implementation will be numbers `x` with `a <= x && x <= b`, and these indeterministic samples will seem to have equal likelihood (hence the name _uniform_) and no sequential pattern with repeated calls to the sampler.

<Statement {...uniform} />

<Statement {...uniformRemark} />

We can test that our `Uniform` distribution indeed samples points in a fashion that maximizes entropy across the interval $[a, b]$.

<Uniform />

```
{../code/src/sampling/examples.rs:25..31}
```

## Exponential measure

<Statement {...exponential} />

The implementation of `Exponential` uses `Uniform` to obtain a sample between $(0, 1)$ and map it via the function $u \mapsto -\frac{1}{\lambda}\log(u) \in [0, \infty)$.
Note that the mapping function returns only positive values, so `Exponential` is a probability measure on $(0, \infty)$.
Due to the concavity of the logarithm, the `Exponential` measure tends to accumulate samples near the origin.

<Exponential />

```
{../code/src/sampling/examples.rs:34..40}
```

Note how the accumulation of samples near the origin is inversely proportional to $\lambda$.
To see this, note that for $\alpha > 0$, a sample from `Exponential` with rate $\alpha \lambda$ is the same as scaling a sample from `Exponential` with rate $\lambda$ by $1/\alpha$.

$$
  -\frac{1}{\alpha\lambda} \log(u) = \frac{1}{\alpha} \Big(-\frac{1}{\lambda}\log(u) \Big)
$$

## Gaussian measure

<Statement {...gaussian} />

We are seeing a pattern develop, where `Gaussian` too samples using the implementation of `Uniform`.
Given _two_ samples from `Uniform` on $(0, 1)$, the implementation of `Gaussian` transforms them via the map $(u_0, u_1) \mapsto \mu + \sqrt{-2\sigma\log(u_0)} \cos(2\pi u_1)$.

<Gaussian />

```
{../code/src/sampling/examples.rs:43..49}
```

Note samples of `Gaussian` accumulate around the mean $\mu$ with distances monotonically related to $\sigma$.
This can be supported by the fact that sampling a `Gaussian` with mean $\mu$ and standard-deviation $\sigma$ is the same thing as applying the operation $x \mapsto \mu + \sqrt\sigma x$ to a sample from `Gaussian` with mean $0$ and standard-deviation $1$.

$$
  \mu + \sqrt{-2\sigma\log(u_0)} \cos(2\pi u_1) = \mu + \sqrt\sigma \Big( \sqrt{-2\log(u_0)} \cos(2\pi u_1) \Big)
$$
